Based on my research:

**Dependency/setup comparison (6.0.2 → 6.0.4):** Diffed the POMs — identical dependency graph, only version numbers bumped (spring-aop/beans/core/expression, all sibling-versioned). Diffed the jar class lists — the only addition is `KotlinReflectionBeanRegistrationAotProcessor`, a `BeanRegistrationAotProcessor` used only by Spring's AOT bean-registration pipeline for Kotlin apps; it's gated behind Kotlin presence (the existing log already shows `KotlinDetector#kotlinPresent set to false at build time`), so it's inert for this Java-only TCK suite. No new runtime dependency, no new Docker requirement, no new system property is implied by the version bump itself.

**Existing scaffold already carries the needed extras.** The 6.0.2 test project already has non-default setup: `org.glassfish:javax.ejb:3.1` and `assertj-core` test deps, `spring.objenesis.ignore=true` system property, and a build-time CGLIB-proxy-class pregeneration task (avoiding runtime bytecode generation under Native Image). Since Forge generates new-version scaffolds from the nearest existing version, these carry forward automatically — nothing new is needed for 6.0.4 specifically.

**Unsupportable-mechanism check:** the issue's log is truncated right after native image build succeeds (analysis/build/image creation all completed); the actual runtime failure text isn't present in the evidence given. Per the required FAILURE step, I can't name a failing operation from the issue alone — so this cannot be an unsupportable verdict regardless of what LoadTimeWeaver/CGLIB code exists (and those existing tests already pass under 6.0.2 without doing real bytecode transformation — they mock the transformer rather than invoking it).

